    "role": "user",
    "content": "\"For each item below, fetch its source and check that the recorded quote appears on the page and supports the item. A card item is a field of the plan in the producer's words, with the sources it cites: check each factual claim in it against those sources, and any value it carries against the page; what the plan will build, measure or predict needs no source, but a factual claim with no source is UNSUPPORTED. For a cost input, check that the page states the number for the setting given; whether it applies to this plan is not yours to judge. When the page states a different number for a num or rate item, give it as page_value. Decide only from the pages you fetch and the text below; do not read files. Return one status per item, under its ref: SUPPORTED, PARTIAL, UNSUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED or UNREACHABLE, with the page sentence you relied on.\nItems, one per line:\np1:step: Voyager Therapeutics — TRACER capsid discovery and ALPL NeuroShuttle (VCAP-102 / VY1706) has this step in its pipeline: Mechanism-of-action step of the TRACER pipeline the card targets: after RNA-guided in vivo capsid evolution, Voyager identifies the vascular receptor each capsid uses to cross the BBB and demonstrates that binding of that receptor initiates transcytosis rather than mere uptake — i.e., the transcytose-versus-retain call per capsid–receptor pair. In practice this is done with human surface-protein binding/transduction screens, SPR, and a polarized cell-barrier Transwell assay, not with exhaustive structural enumeration. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 | recorded quote: \\\"We identify alkaline phosphatase (ALPL) as the primary receptor used by VCAP-102 to cross the BBB and demonstrate that direct binding of VCAP-102 to human ALPL can initiate receptor-mediated transcytosis in a cell barrier model.\\\"\np1:activity: Active as of 2026: the lead program VY1706 is a TRACER-derived capsid gene therapy with AAIC 2026 NHP GLP toxicology data and dosing in early Alzheimer's disease expected in Q4 2026; ALPL-VYGR-NeuroShuttle sits in the wholly-owned research pipeline. | source: https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/pipeline-overview/ | recorded quote: \\\"At AAIC 2026, Voyager presented 6-month good laboratory practice (GLP) toxicology non-human primate (NHP) data showing VY1706 was well tolerated and resulted in sustained tau protein reduction by up to 75% in key AD brain regions following a single intravenous (IV) dose.\\\"\np1:c1: Scale of the discovery funnel feeding the receptor step: TRACER-NHP libraries start at ~4E7 individual capsid variants, narrowed by two in vivo macaque passages to ~1,500 variants of interest before receptor work. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 | recorded quote: \\\"After the initial library cloning and virus production, the naive library contained approximately 4E7 individual capsid variants as identified by next generation sequencing (NGS) (Figure 1 A, step 1).\\\"\np1:c2: Receptor identification is done experimentally with two orthogonal screens (human surface-protein binding microarray and ORFeome transduction screen), not by complex modeling. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 | recorded quote: \\\"For this, we undertook two orthogonal approaches, a capsid binding screen using the Retrogenix cell microarray of human surface proteins and a transduction screen using cells stably transduced with a lentiviral human ORFeome library (Figures S6 A and S6B).\\\"\np1:c3: The transcytose-versus-retain call is made in a polarized cell-barrier (MDCK Transwell) model: human ALPL expression raises VCAP-102 transcytosis >100-fold while AAV9 is unaffected. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 | recorded quote: \\\"Ectopic expression of human ALPL induced a dramatic >100-fold increase in the transcytosis of VCAP-102 while it had no measurable impact on AAV9 migration (Figure 4 F).\\\"\np1:c4: Voyager already works with predicted capsid–receptor interface residues and treats cross-species conservation of the contact set as its translatability argument, so interface-signature outputs are directly consumable. | source: https://www.voyagertherapeutics.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/PRESENTATION_Voyager_IntravenousDeliveryofVY1706_May_2026_Final.pdf | recorded quote: \\\"100% conservation of predicted capsid-ALPL interacting residues\\\"\np1:done: Voyager has run the closest analogue — receptor ID plus a cell-barrier transcytosis demonstration for one capsid family (VCAP-102/ALPL), SPR affinity, cryo-EM showing the insert at the 3-fold apex, and predicted capsid–ALPL contact residues — but has not published exhaustive pose-depth sampling across a capsid panel with transcytose-versus-retain labels. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(25)00373-9 | recorded quote: \\\"We show that ectopic expression of ALPL in a polarized cell layer is sufficient to mediate transcytosis of VCAP-102 and provide in vivo evidence that capsid-receptor interaction is directly correlated with brain transduction.\\\"\np2:step: Gradinaru Lab (Caltech) / APPRAISE-AAV — receptor-directed AAV engineering and the open-source APPRAISE package has this step in its pipeline: The computational step the card scales: ranking engineered capsid insertion peptides against BBB receptors by AlphaFold-Multimer pairwise competition modeling, scored with atom-counting energy plus binding-angle and pocket-depth geometry, used to classify receptor-dependent vs receptor-independent capsids and to pick candidates before in vivo selection. Today this runs at ten models per competition on single-GPU Colab sessions; the card asks whether 144–2,880 pose models per pair change the transcytosis call. | source: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE | recorded quote: \\\"Automated Pair-wise Peptide-Receptor binding model AnalysIs for Screening Engineered proteins (APPRAISE) is a method that predicts the receptor binding propensity of engineered proteins based on high-precision protein structure prediction tools, such as AlphaFold2-multimer.\\\"\np2:activity: Active: the APPRAISE repository received commits on Apr 9, 2026 (1.3.0 package and Boltz-1/Chai-1/OpenFold3 compatibility), and the lab published human CA-IV-targeted capsid AAV-hCA4-IV77 in Cell Reports on Oct 31, 2025. | source: https://github.com/GradinaruLab/APPRAISE | recorded quote: \\\"Current APPRAISE supports backend-specific input generation for legacy ColabFold/AlphaFold-style FASTA files as well as newer open-source multimer tools including Boltz-1, Chai-1, and OpenFold3.\\\"\np2:c1: Standard depth of pose sampling is ten AF-Multimer models per pairwise competition (five predictions per input order, two orders), averaged into a round-robin ranking matrix. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(24)00219-3 | recorded quote: \\\"Consequently, when AlphaFold-Multimer was used, 10 independent models were generated for each competition because AlphaFold-Multimer gives five different predictions for each input, while ESMFold produced two different models for each competition.\\\"\np2:c2: Scale and cost of the step today: a 100-capsid HT-APPRAISE screen completed in 24 h on three parallel Colab GPU sessions, with ~0.1–1 GPU minute per peptide–receptor model and sub-second CPU scoring. | source: https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/molecular-therapy/fulltext/S1525-0016(24)00219-3 | recorded quote: \\\"The HT-APPRAISE screening took 24 h using three parallel Google Colaboratory GPU sessions and a laptop computer.\\\"\np2:c3: Applied to LY6A, LY6C1 and CA4, the 10-model ranking recovered validated binders but produced documented false negatives — the exact failure mode deeper pose sampling is meant to fix. | source: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10115422/ | recorded quote: \\\"Some false negatives were also observed, however, as in 9P08 with LY6A or 9P36 with CA-IV.\\\"\np2:c4: Where full complex modeling has been run (LRP6–capsid VR-VIII), the lab used only five AF-Multimer models with up to 20 recycles on a single A100, and validated poses experimentally and on macaque/human primary brain microvascular endothelial cells. | source: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-52149-0 | recorded quote: \\\"Five structure models were produced using a protocol with up to 20 recycles, and MSA generated with MMseqs2 (UniRef+Environmental) and templates from PDB70.\\\"\np2:done: This lab produced the closest prior work: APPRAISE matrices at ten AF-Multimer models per competition across LY6A/LY6C1/CA4 pairings, a validated PHP.eB–LY6A pose used as the reference anchor, five-model LRP6 complex models with cryo-ET, and observed false negatives — but it has not run pose-depth sweeps (144–2,880) or tested a shared 3-fold GPI motif against transcytosis-versus-retention phenotype labels. | source: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10115422/ | recorded quote: \\\"Each model was recycled three times, and 10 models were generated from each competition.\\\"\np3:step: Dyno Therapeutics — multi-mechanism BBB-crossing capsid portfolio (ALPL, TfR1, Dyno-discovered receptors) has this step in its pipeline: The step the card targets is Dyno's mechanism assignment and structure-aware design for its CNS portfolio: deciding which BBB receptor each engineered capsid engages and whether that engagement crosses the BBB, then engineering capsids for several BBB-crossing mechanisms in parallel (ALPL, hTfR1, and Dyno-discovered receptors) rather than one pathway. Dyno states it integrates sequence- and structure-based approaches in its generative design, with receptor mechanism confirmed by head-to-head in vivo comparisons. | source: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-launches-dyno-yp2-a-top-performing-tfr1-mediated-aav-capsid-to-further-diversify-cns-delivery-portfolio | recorded quote: \\\"Dyno's novel generative AI models, trained on billions of in vivo measurements, most recently accelerated with NVIDIA hardware and software, using Hopper GPUs on DGX Cloud and libraries such as TransformerEngine, integrate sequence- and structure-based approaches to engineer functional capsids for new and promising BBB-crossing mechanisms with exceptional efficiency.\\\"\np3:activity: Active: Dyno launched the ALPL-targeting Dyno-9zh capsid in 2026 and the TfR1-targeting Dyno-yp2 on Jan 12, 2026, with further capsid launches at ASGCT 2026, and runs active Roche/Novartis/Sarepta/Astellas partnerships. | source: https://www.dynotx.com/gene-delivery | recorded quote: \\\"Launched in 2026, the Dyno-9zh is a top-performing capsid that achieves both exceptional brain transduction with significant liver detargeting in NHPs and mouse.\\\"\np3:c1: Dyno deliberately portfolios multiple BBB-crossing mechanisms and treats receptor mechanism as a translatability lever, so a cross-capsid binding signature for transcytosis would sit directly on its decision path. | source: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-launches-dyno-yp2-a-top-performing-tfr1-mediated-aav-capsid-to-further-diversify-cns-delivery-portfolio | recorded quote: \\\"In parallel with its work on TfR1-mediated delivery, Dyno is advancing capsids that engage additional BBB transport pathways, including several BBB-receptors discovered by Dyno, as well as approaches involving alkaline phosphatase (ALPL), a compelling and distinct mechanism with translational relevance across species.\\\"\np3:c2: Its ALPL mechanism (the same GPI-anchored receptor class as the card's motif hypothesis) is validated phenotypically by head-to-head in vivo comparisons of ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP. | source: https://www.dynotx.com/gene-delivery | recorded quote: \\\"Best capsid when compared head-to-head with other ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP.\\\"\np3:c3: Scale of the experimental funnel that any in silico signature would filter: LEAP with billions of in vivo sequence–function measurements per month, aimed at CNS partners such as Roche ($50M upfront, Oct 2024). | source: https://www.dynotx.com/news/dyno-therapeutics-forms-new-strategic-partnership-with-roche-to-advance-aav-gene-therapy-vectors-for-neurological-diseases | recorded quote: \\\"Dyno's Low-Shot Efficient Accelerated Performance (LEAP SM) technology, paired with the capability to make billions of in vivo sequence-function measurements each month, enables optimization of capsids for therapeutic success at an accelerated rate.\\\"\np3:done: No public record shows Dyno has run exhaustive capsid–receptor pose enumeration: its published practice validates receptor mechanisms in vivo (head-to-head ALPL-targeting capsids in mouse and NHP) and folds structure-based approaches into generative design, but any pose-depth studies remain proprietary and unverifiable. | source: https://www.dynotx.com/gene-delivery | recorded quote: \\\"Engages human Alkaline Phosphatase (ALPL) receptor for BBB-crossing, supporting translation to humans.\\\"\nReturn only JSON: {\\\"items\\\": [{\\\"ref\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"status\\\": \\\"SUPPORTED | PARTIAL | UNSUPPORTED | CONTRADICTED | UNREACHABLE\\\", \\\"page_quote\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"note\\\": \\\"\\\"}]}\""
